text:
Come into my little home, where all is clean;

My best things are spread out on the floor to you ;

I want thee to love and revere thy mother dear,

Who loves thee with her heart in every true endeavor.

I am a cloud with nothing underneath me;
 A little child which does not know its mother;
 The voice of God alone is on my forehead;
 With Him my tongue is like unto dumbness;
 My body trembles as a withered leaf;
 I shall never find a rest for my spirit;

Some sweet and tender voice is chanting near,
 Some little children sing a Christmas carol;
 They are glad that Jesus Christ is born again;
 For He came down from Heaven for man’s salvation.

I cannot choose but see Thee near,
 Thou hast such beauty everywhere;
 For everywhere is there a sign
 Of that sweet smile which Thou dost wear;
 And everywhere Thy dear light shineth

She comes on foot, in white, and is not great;
But her small head, though it is not too small,
Is crowned with such an air of triumph!

By one foot is she sitting on a stone;
Her other hand holds the child on her knee;
One eye is fixed upon her infant son,
The other sees the lady and doth moan.

"He had a little room over his head;
He never slept at all in that little bed;
For he could think of no one else but dead
When it grew late for him to sleep alone."

Both are from "Dead Man's Blues," published 1895, as part of a collection called "The Haunted House."

"In some quiet room my mother lay;
The long day had dragged away;
She sank at last into a sleep
That made no sound but moans of deep.
No words could tell what I felt then;
My grief was dumb, and mute as stone."

This is the kind of thing that comes up in this discussion thread all the time. It doesn’t have to mean anything more than it does, and can certainly mean something less than it actually says. If you don’t like these pieces, you shouldn’t read them; but don’ t say

The ship is sailing with her sails furled;
She glides in silence as she moves away;
Her flag no longer flaps upon its staff
Above the bowsprit on the prow.
I cannot see it, but my mind knows well
That out before us lies the world unknown;
Our course lies westward now, where seas are deep
And storms may beat our vessel to death.

I hear an angel's voice — and I am stirred :
It calls me upward — leads me onward still ;
Bids me forget the past— a happier word
Is sweeter music for my yearning ear ;
Tells me " this life shall soon be o'er ; your prayer ,

The old world died, I know not how;
And yet some day it will return,
For all its vanished beauty now!
